## 3. Conclusion
We developed a novel strategy for the synthesis of chemically robust Sq-zbr-MOFs for electrochemical OER reaction. Bimetallic Sq-zbr-MOFs with a zbr topology serve as an excellent platform for electrochemical OER owing to their open pore structure, presence of catalytically active 1D metal hydroxide strips, and high chemical resistance. We showed that regulation of the $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ content in the bimetallic Sq-zbr-MOF system is the key to achieve electrochemical structural stability toward OER. After testing various metal ratios, $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}_{1}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}_{1}$ Sq-zbr-MOFs were demonstrated to exhibit the optimal metal ratios for electrochemical OER in terms of catalytic activity and structural stability. $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}_{1}$ Sq-zbr-MOFs reveals a low overpotential of $230~\mathrm{mV}$ (at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ ) and a small Tafel slope of $37.7\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ , with excellent long-term electrochemical stability for OER. Remarkably, $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}_{1}$ Sq-zbr-MOF outperforms commercially available noble-metal-based $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ catalysts for the OER under identical operational conditions. Our studies demonstrate that chemically robust MOFs with metal hydroxide chains can be considered as a promising structural alternative to metal hydroxide/metal oxides in electrocatalysis.
## 4. Experimental Section
Synthesis of Monometallic and Bimetallic Squarate MOFs: Sq-MOFs based on metal (II) (metal $(11)={\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ or $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ ) were synthesized in a $700~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined reactor under hydrothermal reaction conditions at $220^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . For the pristine monometallic MOF synthesis, a mixture of 3,4-dihydroxy-3-cyclobutene-1,2-dione (squaric acid) $(7.29\ m m)$ , 1.5 eq.), metal (II) chloride hydrate $(4.86~m m)$ , 1 eq.) $(\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{C o}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ or $\mathsf{N i}^{2+})$ ), potassium hydroxide (19.4 mmol, 4 eq.), and water $(77.5~\mathsf{m L})$ was transferred to a $700~\mathsf{m L}$ Teflon-lined reactor. After $30~\mathsf{m i n}$ of sonication, the Teflon-lined reactor was transferred to a preheated oven and heated to $220^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $72\mathrm{~h~}$ . After the reaction, the reactor was slowly cooled and Sq-MOF crystals were isolated using decantation and centrifugation. The Sq-MOF was repeatedly washed with D. I. water, exchanged with acetone and dried. Activation at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ was performed before gas adsorption measurements. For synthesizing bimetallic Sq-MOFs, the following ratios of mix metal ions were used M1: ${\sf M}2=1:3$ $(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{I}=$ $1.275~\mathsf{m m o l}$ , $\mathsf{M}2\ =\ 3.645\ \mathsf{m m o l}.$ ), M1: ${\sf M}2=1:2$ $(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{1}=\mathsf{1}.62$ mmol, $\mathsf{M}2=3.24\mathrm{\mmol}$ ), M1: ${\sf M}2=1:7$ $(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{I}=2.43\ \mathsf{m m o l},$ , $\mathsf{M}2=2.43~\mathsf{m m o l}$ ), M1: $M2=2:7$ $\mathrm{(Ml=3.24~mmol}$ , $\mathsf{M}2=7.62~\mathsf{m m o l}$ ), and $M_{7}$ : ${{\sf M}_{2}}=3{:}7$ $(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{l}=$ $3.645~\mathsf{m m o l}$ , $\mathsf{M}2=7.275~\mathsf{m m o l}$ ).
Preparation of Sq-MOF Electrodes: The working electrode was prepared as follows. To make a uniform electrocatalyst ink, $8~\mathsf{m g}$ of grinded MOF electrocatalyst was mixed with $2~\mathsf{m g}$ of nafion binder (in $1m L$ of $N$ -methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP) and ultrasonicated for $\textsf{l h}$ . The electrocatalyst ink of ${\sf100\mu L}$ was uniformly casted on carbon paper with an active area of $\mathsf{1c m}^{2}$ , followed by drying at $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under vacuum while maintaining an electrocatalyst loading of ${\approx}0.8~\mathsf{m g c m}^{-2}$ .
Electrochemical OER Measurements: Three electrode electrochemical setup (CHI Inc., 760E) with $H_{\mathsf{g}}{\mathsf{-H}}\mathsf{g}\mathsf{O}$ as a reference electrode and a Pt wire as a counter electrode in $\mathsf{1.0~m~}\mathsf{K O H}$ electrolyte was used for electrochemical analyses at room temperature. The working electrode was prepared with a carbon paper $(1\times3~{\mathsf{c m}})$ coated with an electrocatalyst $(1\times1~\mathsf{c m})$ . All the electrode potential was then converted to reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) scale using the following equation: $E_{(\mathsf{R H E})}=E_{(\mathsf{e x p e r i m e n t a l})(\mathsf{H g}/\mathsf{H g}\mathsf{O})}+0.0591\mathsf{\Tilde{\ast}p}\mathsf{H}+E_{0(\mathsf{H g}/\mathsf{H g}\mathsf{O})}$ because $E_{0(\mathsf{H g/H g O})}=0.098$ V versus NHE and 1.0 m KOH or ${\mathsf{p H}}=14$ .
iR correction $(700\%)$ was performed for all CVs and overpotentials using the EIS measured before the electrocatalysis. All CVs and LSVs were then recorded at a scan rate of $5~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}~\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ . Overpotential was determined by $(\eta)=E_{(\tt R H E)}-1.23\vee,$ where $E_{(\mathsf{R H E})}$ was measured at a current density of $10\ m\mathsf{A}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ (unless mentioned especially).
DFT Calculations: All DFT calculations were performed using the Vienna ab initio simulation package (Version: 5.4.4).[22] Projector augmented wave (PAW) pseudopotentials were obtained using the energy cutoff of $650~\mathrm{eV}.$ The Perdew−Burke−Ernzerhof (PBE) generalized gradient approximations (GGA) were used to describe the exchange− correlation functional.[23] Grimme semiempirical DFT-D3 van der Waals force dispersion correction was used for all calculations.[24] Coulombic interactions were introduced using the $\mathsf{G C A+}U$ scheme to describe the strong correlation effects in Fe and Ni atoms. Hubbard $U$ values for Fe and Ni atoms were obtained from previous studies (5.20 and $6.40\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively).[25] The structure models of Ni- and $\mathsf{N i}_{2}$ Fe Sq-MOF $(\mathbb{1}\times\mathbb{1}\times2)$ supercells with a $k.$ -mesh of $3\times3\times2$ grid in reciprocal space were used to identify the OER activity sites. An implicit solvation method, as implemented in the VASPsol package, was used to consider solvation effect in the reaction.[26] Bader charge analysis was used to decompose the charge density.[21] Chemical bonding analysis was performed using the LOBSTER package.[20b] Moreover, additional details of the calculations, i.e., entropy, zero-point energy corrections, and COHP have been reported in the Supporting Information section.
